is  designed  to be a compact representation of the C programming
language’s type information focused on serving the needs  of  dy‐
namic  tracing,  debuggers, and other in‐situ and post‐mortem in‐
trospection tools.  data is generally included in objects and  is
tagged  as  to  ensure  that  the data is accessible in a running
process and in subsequent core dumps,  if  generated.   The  data
contained in each file has information about the layout and sizes
of  C types, including intrinsic types, enumerations, structures,
typedefs, and unions, that are used by the corresponding  object.
The  data  may also include information about the types of global
objects and the return type and arguments  of  functions  in  the
symbol  table.   Because  a file is often embedded inside a file,
rather than being a standalone file itself, it may  also  be  re‐
ferred  to  as a On illumos systems, data is consumed by multiple
programs.  It can be used by Programmatic access to data  can  be
obtained  through  The file format is broken down into seven dif‐
ferent sections.  The first section is the  and  which  describes
the  version  of  the  file, links it has to other files, and the
sizes of the other sections.  The next section  is  the  section,
which provides a way of identifying similar groups of data across
multiple  files.   This  is  followed by the information section,
which describes the type of global symbols.  The subsequent  sec‐
tion is the information section, which describes the return types
and  arguments of functions.  The next section is the information
section, which describes the format and layout  of  the  C  types
themselves,  and  finally  the last section is the section, which
contains the names of types, enumerations, members,  and  labels.
While  strictly  speaking, only the and are required, to be actu‐
ally useful, both the type and string sections are necessary.   A
file may contain all of the type information that it requires, or
it may optionally refer to another file which holds the remaining
types.   When a file refers to another file, it is called the and
the file it refers to is called the A given file may  only  refer
to  one  parent.   This process is called because it ensures each
child only has type information that is unique to it.   A  common
example  of  this  is  that  most  kernel  modules in illumos are
uniquified against the kernel module  and  the  type  information
that  comes  from  the module.  This means that a module only has
types that are unique to itself and the most common types in  the
kernel  are  not  duplicated.  This documents version of the file
format.  All applications and tools currently produce and operate
on this version.  The file format can be summarized with the fol‐
